RISC-V: a zero-extending INT->LONG cast of a value known non-negative becomes a sign-extending one
(applies after perf-43).

Assertion prop turns a sign-extending cast of a non-negative INT into a zero-extending one, which is
cheaper on x64 and arm64; perf-43 disabled that on RISC-V, where the sign extension is usually free.
This adds the reverse: optAssertionProp_Cast clears GTF_UNSIGNED on an int->long cast whose operand
optAssertionProp_RangeProperties proves non-negative (VN, bounds-check and relop assertions,
range check). Lowering then drops the cast when the operand register is already sign-extended, so
slli.uw rd, rs, 0 disappears and add.uw / shNadd.uw (two ZisK steps each) become add / shNadd.

Nethermind stateless guest on ZisK, block 26055159 / 26112793:
  steps 56,072,171 -> 55,880,606 (-0.34%) / 131,086,840 -> 130,703,919 (-0.29%)
  cost 9,342,293,548 -> 9,310,608,130 (-0.34%) / 19,294,339,711 -> 19,233,259,159 (-0.32%)
  sll_u_w 321,604 -> 145,663 on the light block.
Outputs identical on 11 mainnet blocks.

diff --git a/src/coreclr/jit/assertionprop.cpp b/src/coreclr/jit/assertionprop.cpp
--- a/src/coreclr/jit/assertionprop.cpp
+++ b/src/coreclr/jit/assertionprop.cpp
@@ -5018,6 +5018,22 @@
         }
     }
 
+#ifdef TARGET_RISCV64
+    // The reverse on RISC-V, where the sign extension is the cheaper one: an INT register is usually
+    // already sign-extended, while the zero extension takes an instruction (or turns add/shNadd into
+    // add.uw/shNadd.uw).
+    if (cast->IsUnsigned() && !cast->gtOverflow() && genActualTypeIsInt(lcl) && cast->TypeIs(TYP_LONG))
+    {
+        bool isKnownNonZero;
+        bool isKnownNonNegative;
+        optAssertionProp_RangeProperties(assertions, lcl, stmt, block, &isKnownNonZero, &isKnownNonNegative);
+        if (isKnownNonNegative)
+        {
+            cast->ClearUnsigned();
+        }
+    }
+#endif // TARGET_RISCV64
+
     if (!optLocalAssertionProp)
     {
         // Decide whether removing this cast (vs only clearing GTF_OVERFLOW) is safe.
